              answer: "An ADHD meltdown is primarily triggered by acute emotional dysregulation, frustration overload, or rejection sensitive dysphoria (RSD)—often sparked when transitions are forced or executive working memory is swamped. An autistic meltdown is more commonly triggered by sensory overstimulation (noise, lights, crowds) or unexpected disruption to routines."
            },
            {
              question: "How does a meltdown tracker or child behaviour diary help parents?",
              answer: "A meltdown tracker records the antecedent (what happened before), physical cues of autonomic arousal, sensory environment, and recovery duration. Tracking 5 to 7 episodes reveals predictable tipping points, allowing parents to co-regulate and intervene before the threshold is breached."
            }
          ]} />

          <PrevNextNav
            prev={{ title: "How to Support Executive Function", href: "/blog/how-to-support-executive-function-at-home" }}
            next={{ title: "Autism Early Signs Parents Should Notice", href: "/blog/autism-early-signs" }}
          />

        <ArticleAuthor
          author="Rabbia Ashraf"
